Looked at how cookies get written out.

**Where the error comes from**

`toResponse()` in `packages/core/src/lib/utils/web.ts` is the only caller that serializes cookies:

```ts
const { name, value, options } = cookie
const cookieHeader = serializeCookie(name, value, options)
```

`serializeCookie` is the vendored copy, whose `serialize()` validates `options.domain` against the RFC 6265 `domain-value` grammar (`packages/core/src/lib/vendored/cookie.ts:282`). That grammar has no `:port` production, so `.acme.internal:8443` fails with `option domain is invalid`. Nothing in core derives `domain` from a URL/host — it's whatever the config (here, the partner's proxy) supplies — so the port has to be stripped before serialization.

**The fix**

Since the vendored file can't be patched, sanitize in its caller. In `toResponse`, drop the port from `options.domain` before passing it to `serializeCookie`:

```ts
res.cookies?.forEach((cookie) => {
  const { name, value, options } = cookie
  // The Domain attribute is a bare hostname; a proxy may hand us
  // e.g. ".acme.internal:8443", which the serializer rejects (leading dot kept).
  const domain = options?.domain?.replace(/:\d+$/, "")
  const cookieHeader = serializeCookie(name, value, { ...options, domain })
  if (headers.has("Set-Cookie")) headers.append("Set-Cookie", cookieHeader)
  else headers.set("Set-Cookie", cookieHeader)
})
```

A `domain == null/undefined` stays `undefined`, so the default host-only behavior is unchanged; only a trailing `:port` is removed. (An alternative is to centralize this in `packages/core/src/lib/utils/cookie.ts` where cookie options are assembled, but that file builds `SessionStore`/defaults rather than serializing the outgoing header, so the single choke point for the failing call is `web.ts`.)

