## Why it throws

The vendored `packages/core/src/lib/vendored/cookie.ts` validates the `Domain` attribute against the RFC 6265 `domain-value` regexp (line ~60), which permits only labels separated by dots — no colon, so `.acme.internal:8443` is rejected:

```ts
if (options.domain) {
  if (!domainValueRegExp.test(options.domain)) {
    throw new TypeError(`option domain is invalid: ${options.domain}`)
  }
}
```

That file is a verbatim upstream copy resynced on every upgrade, so patching it is pointless. The port is also meaningless in a `Domain` attribute anyway — cookies are scoped by host, and the port the proxy lives on is irrelevant to the browser.

## Where to fix

There is exactly one call site that serializes cookies (`serializeCookie`) in core: `toResponse` in **`packages/core/src/lib/utils/web.ts`** (line 79). The `options` it passes come straight from the configured cookie options (i.e. your partner's `cookies.sessionToken.options.domain`), so it's the right boundary to normalize the value before handing it to the vendored serializer:

```ts
res.cookies?.forEach((cookie) => {
  const { name, value, options } = cookie
  const cookieHeader = serializeCookie(name, value, {
    ...options,
    // The `Domain` attribute must be a host name; ports are not part of the
    // cookie scope, and the vendored parser rejects them.
    domain: options?.domain?.replace(/:\d+$/, ""),
  })
  if (headers.has("Set-Cookie")) headers.append("Set-Cookie", cookieHeader)
  else headers.set("Set-Cookie", cookieHeader)
})
```

(`lib/utils/cookie.ts` only defines defaults and `SessionStore` and never sets/forwards a domain to the serializer, and `jwt.ts` only parses cookies, so neither needs to change.)

