## Diagnosis

The `431` isn't really about the cookie chunk size — it's that the session JWT *contains* the group claims. Your session strategy is `jwt` (`packages/core/src/lib/utils/cookie.ts` chunks a token over `CHUNK_SIZE` into `authjs.session-token.0/1/2/...`), and each chunk repeats the cookie name + attributes, so a token with hundreds of group claims fans out into many cookies and the browser sends them all back on every request — right after sign-in that Cookie header exceeds the server/CDN limit. Lowering `CHUNK_SIZE` makes it worse (more cookies, bigger total header — Safari also drops the tail chunks).

## Proposed change

Don't persist group claims in the session token at all. In the app's `callbacks.jwt` (`apps/dev/nextjs/auth.ts`, and the same callback in the enterprise customer's config — note the Entra/FusionAuth examples spread `...account` / the full profile into the token), return only the identity fields you need for authorization:

```ts
callbacks: {
  jwt({ token, user, account, profile }) {
    if (account && user) {
      // id + role only; no `...account`, no `profile.groups`
      token.id = user.id
      token.role = (profile as any)?.roles?.[0] ?? user.role
    }
    return token
  },
},
```

Then resolve group membership server-side where it's actually needed — either via the Entra group-overage path (`hasgroups`/`_claim_names` → `https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/users/{oid}/getMemberObjects`, as the `MicrosoftEntraIDProfile` docs in `packages/core/src/providers/microsoft-entra-id.ts` describe) or a cached server-side lookup — instead of shipping hundreds of group IDs through the browser.

This is the same fix that worked previously on this fork: trimming the JWT to user id + role, plus loading groups on the server.

